Answer: theia is a technique for localizing structural bugs in deep-learning programs—such as issues with activation functions, layer/model properties, loss functions, data preprocessing, or missing/redundant/wrong layers—and suggesting fixes. it uses dataset characteristics and parsed source code, builds a library-independent meta-model, and applies verification rules with context-sensitive analyses to detect bugs in keras and pytorch models early in training. it supports fcnns and cnns for regression and classification.

neuralint is a prior/state-of-the-art bug-detection approach used as a comparison baseline. theia was reported as more effective: it detected 57/75 bugs in 40 real-world buggy dl programs, while neuralint detected 17/75.
